---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = 'https://www.dg-sdk.com';
const url = base + '/blog/surface-particle-sampling-recovery-blanks';
const title = 'Surface Particle Sampling: Recovery Checks and Blanks';
const seoTitle = 'Surface Particle Sampling: Recovery and Blanks';
const description = 'Separate sampling recovery from blank background when reviewing surface particle counts, and define which evidence a dry-cleaning trial needs.';
---

<Layout title={seoTitle + ' | Shidike'} description={description} canonical={url}
  image="/assets/img/blog/sampling-recovery-blank-card-v1.jpg">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: base },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: base + '/blog' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: title, item: url }
    ]
  })} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BlogPosting', headline: title, description,
    image: base + '/assets/img/blog/sampling-recovery-blank-card-v1.jpg',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', url: base },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', legalName: 'Dongguan Shidike Intelligent Equipment Co., Ltd.', url: base },
    datePublished: '2026-11-27', dateModified: '2026-11-27', mainEntityOfPage: url
  })} />

  <article class="post">
    <header class="post-head">
      <div class="container post-col">
        <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>Particle Sampling Recovery and Blanks</div>
        <div class="post-tags"><span class="blog-tag">Surface Cleaning</span><span class="blog-tag">Particle Sampling</span><span class="blog-tag">Measurement Quality</span></div>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="post-lead">A particle count from a collected sample is one step removed from the original surface. Before using it to judge a dry-cleaning trial, ask two different questions: what did collection leave behind, and what did the measurement process introduce? Recovery checks and blanks address those separate questions.</p>
        <div class="post-info"><span>November 27, 2026</span><span>Shidike Technical Team</span><span>3 min read</span></div>
      </div>
    </header>
    <div class="container post-col post-body">
      <picture class="post-figure">
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/sampling-recovery-blank-v1-mobile.webp" type="image/webp" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/sampling-recovery-blank-v1-mobile.jpg" type="image/jpeg" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source srcset="/assets/img/blog/sampling-recovery-blank-v1.webp" type="image/webp" />
        <img src="/assets/img/blog/sampling-recovery-blank-v1.jpg" alt="Two sampling questions: particles left on the surface and background introduced along the measurement path." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </picture>
      <p><em>Original concept diagram. It explains measurement questions, not a sampling instruction, measured recovery fraction or DGSDK test result.</em></p>

      <section id="sampling-recovery">
        <h2>Recovery: what reached the collected sample?</h2>
        <p>In <a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19890003216/downloads/19890003216.pdf?attachment=true" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Peterson and White's optical-surface study, hosted by NASA</a>, the tested removal-and-analysis route underrepresented particles compared with direct observations, particularly at smaller sizes. Collection and detection both affected the reported population.</p>
        <p>This historical experiment used particular surfaces, instruments and liquid-flush sampling. It supports checking recovery; it does not prescribe that method for dry-cleaning trials or supply a correction factor for another material. Ask which surface and particle conditions the laboratory's recovery evidence actually covers.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="blank-scope">
        <h2>A procedural blank needs a defined scope</h2>
        <p>The <a href="https://dev.goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/08010/plain" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IUPAC Gold Book entry for blank material</a> distinguishes a procedural blank carried through the complete measurement procedure from blanks covering particular stages. This is terminology, not a surface-testing recipe.</p>
        <p>For a particle report, ask what was handled, exposed, transported and analyzed in its blank. An instrument-only check cannot stand in for every earlier step. A retained piece of untreated production material is also not automatically a blank: it may contain the very particles being investigated.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="surface-blank-example">
        <h2>Name the control that was actually used</h2>
        <p>A <a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19920014960" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1991 NASA contractor report by Germani</a> compared particles from mirror-surface tape lifts with a control from the portion of a tape sample that had not contacted the mirror. Composition and size comparisons helped distinguish surface findings from sampling or handling background.</p>
        <p>That was a specific non-contact tape control. It should not be relabeled as proof that every measurement stage was covered, or that all surface particles were recovered. A blank need not produce a zero count to be informative; the report must explain what its observation means.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="surface-applicability">
        <h2>Check whether the method suits the surface</h2>
        <p>The public guidance for <a href="https://store.astm.org/e1216-21.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM E1216-21 tape-lift sampling</a> identifies adhesive-transfer and coating-damage concerns and notes reduced removal efficiency on rough surfaces. Its scope leaves part-acceptance criteria to the user.</p>
        <p>A familiar method name therefore does not establish suitability for an unknown optical coating or flexible film. Agree the method and compatibility evidence with the responsible laboratory before sampling. This article does not recommend applying tape or solvent to a customer's material, or claim compliance with the full standard.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="two-checks">
        <h2>Keep recovery and background evidence separate</h2>
        <p>A low blank does not establish complete collection. Recovery evidence does not establish low background. Neither is the cleaning machine's removal efficiency. Use this short record to resolve those distinctions before interpreting the material trial.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Blank recovery and background review record">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Evidence</th><th scope="col">Question for the laboratory</th><th scope="col">Your reference</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Recovery check</th><td>Which surface, particles and collection method were evaluated?</td><td>___</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Blank scope</th><td>Which measurement stages did the control cover?</td><td>___</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Reported result</th><td>What are the raw observation, blank result and any stated correction?</td><td>___</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Keep any correction identifiable and ask the laboratory to justify its use. Do not silently subtract a blank or import a recovery factor from another method.</p>
        <p>For the underlying count, see <a href="/blog/surface-particle-inspection-report">surface-particle inspection reporting</a>. For material identity and trial comparisons, use <a href="/blog/coated-optical-film-cleaning-trial-samples">the coated-film sample guide</a>. The <a href="/guides/what-is-web-cleaning">web-cleaning guide</a> covers process selection.</p>
      </section>
      <div class="post-cta">
        <h2>Plan the trial and its measurement together</h2>
        <p>Share the material, dry-particle contamination and intended surface criterion with Shidike. Include the proposed inspection method and available laboratory records so the cleaning trial has an agreed way to assess its result.</p>
        <p><a class="send-cta" href="/products/cleaning-machine">Explore Cleaning Machines &rarr;</a><a class="send-alt" href="/contact?type=selection&amp;product=Surface%20Particle%20Sampling%20Review">Discuss your cleaning application &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <style>
    .post-col { max-width: 780px; }
    .post-head { background: #f7f8fa; padding: 48px 0 34px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .post-head .breadcrumb { font-size: 13px; color: #7d8d97; margin-bottom: 18px; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb a { color: var(--brand-deep); text-decoration: none; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb span { margin: 0 8px; }
    .post-tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; margin-bottom: 14px; }
    .blog-tag {
      font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase;
      background: #eef4f8; color: #4a6373; padding: 4px 9px; border-radius: 999px;
    }
    .post-head h1 {
      font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3.4vw, 2.5rem); line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 800;
      letter-spacing: -.02em; color: var(--navy); margin: 0 0 14px;
    }
    .post-lead { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #46545f; margin: 0 0 18px; }
    .post-info { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 12.5px; color: #8a99a4; }

    .post-body { padding: 34px 0 70px; }
    .post-figure { display: block; margin: 0 0 30px; }
    .post-figure img {
      display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px;
    }
    .post-body h2 {
      font-size: 1.42rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -.01em; color: var(--navy);
      margin: 34px 0 12px;
    }
    .post-body p { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 15px; }
    .post-body a { color: var(--brand-deep); }
    .post-body h3 {
      font-size: 1.12rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); margin: 26px 0 10px;
    }
    .post-body ul, .post-body ol { margin: 0 0 15px; padding-left: 22px; }
    .post-body li { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 7px; }
    .post-body blockquote, .post-body .blog-quote {
      margin: 0 0 18px; padding: 13px 16px; background: #f2f6f9;
      border-left: 3px solid var(--brand-deep); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    }
    .post-body .blog-quote p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
    .post-body .step-block { margin: 0 0 22px; }
    .post-body .step-num {
      display: inline-block; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em;
      text-transform: uppercase; background: var(--navy); color: #fff;
      padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 999px; margin-bottom: 8px;
    }
    .formula {
      font-size: 1.2rem !important; font-weight: 700; text-align: center; color: var(--navy) !important;
      background: #f2f6f9; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px;
    }

    .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 0 20px; }
    .post-body table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14.5px; }
    .post-body th, .post-body td {
      text-align: left; padding: 10px 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); vertical-align: top;
    }
    .post-body thead th { background: #f2f6f9; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); }
    .post-body tbody th { width: 22%; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); background: #fafbfc; }

    .faq-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; margin: 6px 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item { padding: 15px 4px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); font-size: 15.5px; }
    .faq-item p { margin: 10px 0 0; }

    .post-cta {
      margin-top: 40px; padding: 26px 26px 22px; background: #f7f8fa;
      border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 10px;
    }
    .post-cta h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.25rem; }
    .send-cta, .send-alt { display: inline-block; font-weight: 700; margin-right: 22px; }
    @media (max-width: 767px) {
      .post-body { padding-left: 16px; padding-right: 16px; }
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
